/*! \page dapp_release_provisioning dApp v1 Release-Key Provisioning

For the full package authoring and DU enablement walkthrough, see
\ref dapp_quickstart. This page is only the offline release-key custody,
immutable catalog generation, rollover, and revocation runbook.

\section dapp_release_scope Scope and version rule

This runbook provisions the existing OCUDU dApp package protocol. Every
OCUDU-owned contract remains version 1 until a formal release decision:
manifest, detached signature, trust policy, ASN.1 management service, ABI,
FlatBuffers publication envelope, and provisioning report. New optional fields
must preserve the v1 compatibility rules. The installed authoring SDK and
OCUDU-owned reference packages likewise report version 1; finer-grained release
numbers are intentionally deferred until a formal release. \c SPDX-2.3
identifies the external SPDX document format and is not an OCUDU
protocol-version change.

The DU never holds a signing private key, contacts a signing service, downloads
a trust root, or changes trust policy through E3. It reads a locally
provisioned, immutable v1 trust policy during startup and verifies the exact
manifest, SPDX SBOM, and artifact before preflight or execution. This keeps
offline release authority outside the latency and failure domain of the modem.

\section dapp_release_roles Roles and custody boundary

Use at least two named human roles for a production ceremony:

- A release builder produces immutable artifacts, manifests, and SPDX SBOMs,
  but does not possess the release private key.
- A key custodian controls the offline signing environment and authorizes the
  monotonically increasing package sequence and trust-policy epoch.
- A deployment operator receives only public catalog material and the approved
  trust policy, installs it atomically, and records the configured minimum
  policy epoch.
- A reviewer independently compares package IDs, digests, sequence floors,
  key IDs, and ceremony evidence before deployment.

The v1 reference tool uses an unencrypted PEM key with mode 0600 so that the
file admission and protocol can be tested without a runtime dependency. For a
real release, keep that file on encrypted offline media or an equivalently
controlled ephemeral signing host, never in source control, CI artifacts,
shell history, a DU image, or an E3 response. HSM-backed signing can later be
added behind the offline tool without changing the v1 sidecar or DU verifier.
The ceremony must define two-person access, inventory, encrypted backup,
recovery testing, media destruction, incident response, and an audit-retention
period under the organization's security policy.

\section dapp_release_initial Initial provisioning

1. Install the \c dapp_sdk CMake component into a clean staging prefix. Use the
   installed \c bin/ocudu-dapp-package and the schemas beneath
   \c share/ocudu/dapp/schema; do not qualify a build-tree-only tool.
2. Verify modes, ownership, and SHA-256 for the installed tool and all v1
   schemas. Generate the offline P-256 key pair into an already protected
   directory. Record only the public-key ID.
3. Verify every SDK-produced manifest and digest-bound SPDX SBOM, then sign the
   exact read-only manifest at a nonzero, never-reused package sequence.
4. Build a v1 trust policy at a nonzero monotonic epoch. It contains the active
   public key and one minimum sequence for every admitted package ID.
5. Use \c inspect with the deployment's minimum policy epoch for every package.
   Copy the catalog and trust policy to a new deployment generation, fsync it,
   and atomically change the local startup configuration. E3 may report the
   resulting non-secret signer state, but may not select paths or trust roots.
6. Retain public evidence and securely remove ephemeral private-key material.
   Keep the conventional DU fallback and the prior public catalog generation
   until the new generation has completed ordinary bootstrap and lifecycle
   validation.

Never overwrite a signature sidecar in place. The tool deliberately uses
exclusive creation. Stage a new immutable catalog generation and promote the
directory or configuration reference atomically.

\section dapp_release_rollover Two-key rollover

Rollover is three monotonic public-policy steps, not a key-file replacement:

1. Publish a higher-epoch overlap policy containing both old and new public
   keys while retaining the old package floors. Both the old catalog and the
   newly signed higher-sequence catalog must inspect successfully.
2. Deploy and validate the new-key catalog. All package sequences increase;
   none is reused, even when artifact content is unchanged.
3. Publish another higher-epoch final policy containing the public keys needed
   for attribution, listing the old key ID as revoked, and raising each package
   floor to its new sequence. Set the DU's configured minimum epoch to this
   final value. Verify that the new catalog passes, the old key is rejected as
   revoked, below-floor new-key packages are rejected, and the overlap policy
   is rejected as an epoch rollback.

Revocation always wins over key presence. Do not lower an epoch or package
floor to recover a failed deployment. Restore service with a newly signed,
higher-sequence package and a same-or-higher policy epoch. If the new key is
suspected, stop signing, preserve public evidence, generate a distinct key in
the approved offline environment, and execute another rollover with the
compromised key explicitly revoked.

\section dapp_release_qualification Installed-flow evidence

The registered test
\c dapp_dummy_ru_e2e.installed_release_provisioning_v1 installs only the
\c dapp_sdk component into a clean prefix, validates the installed tool and
schemas, signs all six CPU reference packages with old and new keys, exercises
overlap, revocation, sequence-floor and epoch-rollback outcomes, removes the
temporary private-key paths, and admits the final rotated catalog through the
ordinary dummy-RU bootstrap and E3 lifecycle. It emits a strict
\c ocudu.dapp.release-provisioning-report.v1 document validated by the
installed \c release-provisioning-report-v1.schema.json.

Run the unchanged test on every declared release architecture and preserve one
report per exact build and platform. A passing Arm64 report does not qualify
x86-64, another libc/toolchain, CUDA, or a vendor SoC. The software exercise
proves installed packaging and cryptographic state transitions; it does not by
itself approve organizational custody, physical controls, HSM operation, or
production timing. Those remain explicit release gates.

*/
